## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical data isolation, policy enforcement, and automated remediation in a multi-tenant AWS setup.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-prefix isolation in S3
- IAM condition keys (aws:SourceAccount, aws:SourceArn)
- Per-tenant KMS keys and grants
- Service Controls Policies to prevent cross-tenant writes
- Config-based drift detection and EventBridge-backed remediation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example bucket resource policy (conceptual)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::TENANT_ACCOUNT:role/TenantIngress"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-lake/tenant-prefix-*/**",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "s3:prefix": "tenant-prefix-*"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cross-tenant data leakage exit paths?
- How would you roll back a misconfigured bucket policy without downtime?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design enterprise-grade multi-tenant security architectures using AWS KMS, addressing real-world requirements for data sovereignty, tenant isolation, and key management in SaaS environments.

## Key Concepts
- Customer Managed Keys (CMKs) and tenant control
- Cross-account KMS access patterns
- Envelope encryption architecture
- Key rotation and revocation mechanisms
- Audit trails and compliance verification
- Incident response for key compromise

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::SERVICE-ACCOUNT:role/ServicePrincipal"
      },
      "Action": [
        "kms:Encrypt",
        "kms:Decrypt",
        "kms:GenerateDataKey*"
      ],
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringEquals": {
          "kms:CallerAccount": "SERVICE-ACCOUNT"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of cross‑account access, least privilege, and data isolation in S3 with AWS IAM roles and bucket policies. It also touches on revocation and testing in a real-world partner scenario.

## Key Concepts
- Cross‑account IAM trust and role policies
- S3 bucket policies and per‑prefix access
- Least privilege and access revocation
- Private access options (VPC endpoint) and public access controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Trust policy (role in data account)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<PartnerAcct>:root"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Role policy: s3:GetObject on partner prefix
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-bucket/partner-prefix/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

```javascript
// Bucket policy: allow role to read
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<DataAcct>:role/PartnerReadRole"},
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::data-bucket/partner-prefix/*"
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit access and rotation history for the partner?
- What failure modes or revocation delays could occur, and how to mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design secure, multi-tenant data architectures with proper cryptographic isolation, cross-account access patterns, and safe deployment practices for sensitive analytics workloads.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant KMS CMK isolation with restrictive key policies
- Cross-account access using STS AssumeRole
- Tenant-specific masking rule storage and retrieval
- Blue-green deployments and canary testing for safe rollouts

## Code Example
```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "Allow central analytics plane decrypt",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {
        "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::CENTRAL_ACCOUNT_ID:role/AnalyticsPlaneRole"
      },
      "Action": [
        "kms:Decrypt",
        "kms:DescribeKey"
      ],
      "Resource": "arn:aws:kms:*:TENANT_ACCOUNT_ID:key/*",
      "Condition": {
        "ArnLike": {
          "kms:EncryptionContext:TenantId": "arn:aws:kms:*:TENANT_ACCOUNT_ID:key/CMK_ID"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

The key policy uses the `kms:EncryptionContext` condition to ensure the central plane can only decrypt data encrypted under the specific tenant's CMK, preventing cross-tenant data access.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests cross-account log delivery, least privilege, and lifecycle handling in a real SaaS pattern.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-account IAM roles and bucket policies
- TLS-only S3 access and private endpoints
- CMK encryption and CloudTrail validation
- Onboarding/offboarding automation and rollback

## Code Example
```json
// Central bucket policy sketch
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"AWS": "arn:aws:iam::<central-id>:role/TenantLogPublisher"},
      "Action": "s3:PutObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::central-logs-bucket/tenant-prefix/*",
      "Condition": {
        "Bool": {"aws:SecureTransport": "true"}
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

```json
// Trust policy sketch for TenantLogPublisher (in central/account_root)
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": {"Service": "delivery.logs.amazonaws.com"},
      "Action": "sts:AssumeRole",
      "Condition": {"StringEquals": {"aws:SourceAccount": "<central-id>"}}
    }
  ]
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end log delivery across accounts?
- How would you handle rotation of the CMK and rotation of the tenant role?
